    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d170">

                <pb facs="0174" n="162" xml:id="pg162"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.0011 European War 1939/8891: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in the Soviet Union</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Steinhardt</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Moscow</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1941-03-08T14:00:00+03:00">March 8, 1941—2
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received March 8—11:15 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>453. I have learned from a non-British source which I regard as reliable that
                    prior to his departure for Ankara<anchor n="11" xml:id="d170-f1"/> to meet Eden,
                    the British Ambassador informally suggested to the Soviet authorities that Eden
                    might be prepared to visit Moscow, but that they did not encourage the
                    visit.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Steinhardt</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d170-f1" n="11" xml:id="d170-f1-content">
                <p>Sir Stafford Cripps left Moscow on February 26, 1941.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
